# Does the motor mounting interface hold up?
#
# Nothing in this assembly is a part of the arm: it is the check the
# '/pc:add-interfaces' skill asks for before an interface is used anywhere -
# mate two parts through it and look at the result. A port is a coordinate frame
# rather than geometry, so the only way to know whether it is where the feature
# is is to put two parts together by it and see whether the faces meet.
#
# What should be true of the picture:
#   * the spacer sits flat on the motor's output face, concentric with it;
#   * it is not inside the motor, and there is no gap;
#   * its port coincides with the motor's, +Z arrows opposed
#     ('pc render -a -t png --with-internals check/motor-mount').
#
# Only the front spacer is mated here now. The flange was too, on the reading that
# a part carrying the motor's bolt circle bolts to the motor - and the released
# assembly says it does not: see 'flange' in ./partcad.yaml. What is left is the
# one mate the release confirms, and the motor's port is derived from that release
# rather than asserted, so this picture is a check on the arithmetic rather than on
# a guess.

links:
  # The motor by its own name in the package that declares it, and not this
  # package's alias of it: an alias does not carry the source's 'implements:', so
  # its ports are empty and a 'connect:' naming them silently places nothing.
  # See ../../PARTCAD.md.
  - part: //pub/robotics/rebot/devarm/third_party:actuator/dm4340p
    name: motor

  - part: motor-front-spacer
    name: spacer
    connect:
      with: //pub/robotics/rebot/devarm/third_party:actuator/dm43xx-mounted
      name: motor
      to: //pub/robotics/rebot/devarm/third_party:actuator/dm43xx-output
